 "eras": [
  {"id":"1327","year":"1327","label":"Muhammad bin Tughluq's capital","short":"1327","lens":"none","summary":"Devagiri as Muhammad bin Tughluq renamed it Daulatabad and moved his capital there (1327 or 1328): the rock fortress alone.","basis":"Wikipedia: founded c. 1187 by Bhillama V; capital moved in 1327 (one passage says 1328) and returned to Delhi in 1334. Only the OSM hill outline and scarp lines are drawn.","conjecture":"No plan of any period was found. The rock is drawn as mapped today; the ring walls are left out because the page says their builder is uncertain (Malik Ambar or the Tughluqs for Ambarkot).","sources":[],"facts":["Wikipedia: Bhillama V founded Devagiri in about 1187; Alauddin Khalji raided it in 1296; Muhammad bin Tughluq moved the capital in 1327 (one passage says 1328) and back to Delhi in 1334.","The hill is about 200 m high and its lower slopes were scarped to leave about 50 m of vertical rock (Wikipedia)."]},
  {"id":"1633","year":"1633","label":"Mughal capture","short":"1633","lens":"none","summary":"The fort at the Mughal capture (1632 or 1633), shown as the hill plus the present mapped wall lines carried back.","basis":"Wikipedia: Ahmadnagar Sultanate from 1499; Khadki (later Aurangabad) founded nearby in 1610; Mughal capture 1632 or 1633 (the page gives both).","conjecture":"Which of the mapped wall lines existed in 1633, and which ring each is, is not established; none has a source height. All are carried back unchanged, marked inferred.","sources":[],"facts":["Mughal capture is dated 1632 in one passage and 1633 in another (Wikipedia); the Marathas took the fort in 1760.","The outer wall (Ambarkot) is described as elliptical, about 2 km north-south, with two defence walls; the Kataka is a double rampart with bastions and moats (Wikipedia)."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"Ticketed monument","short":"Monument","lens":"none","summary":"A ticketed monument on a rock about 200 m high, with long wall lines, a temple area, a large tank and the Mendha Top and other cannons.","basis":"The OSM hill outline, scarp lines, wall lines, three gate nodes, three cannon markers, Hathi Haud, Hemadpanti temple and the Bharat Mata temple area.","conjecture":"Heights are unknown (kerb-high). The Chand Minar, Chini Mahal, the moat and the dark passage are not mapped and so not drawn.","sources":[],"facts":["The Chand Minar is attributed to Hasan Gangu Bahmani (1347-58) in one passage and dated 1446 in another (Wikipedia); no height is given there.","The fort is listed as owned by the Government of India and open to the public (Wikipedia)."]}
 ],
